Found by mcp-hub-worker during k73 (2026-11-06). After k73 a removed/replaced upstream is freed, but MCP 0.15's to_action sets up a strong server <-> MCP::Server::Transport::HTTP cycle, so any server that was served once -- a façade server or an in-process class instance -- outlives its upstream, with all its tools, schemas and extras. Confirmed in scratch: weakening transport->{server} frees the stdio and fork façades. Fix belongs in the Upstream::action builder (lib/MCP/Hub/Upstream.pm), or upstream in MCP itself (then: a ticket for the MCP dist, not a silent edit). Done: a served-then-removed upstream's server is freed after reload; extend t/reload-free.t (k73) with 'served on its endpoint before the reload' for each type.

From k93 (natives worker probe): the façade server of every upstream type -- stdio, http, perl, fork, docker -- and a native instance served on its own endpoint stay alive after a reload through this cycle (to_action gives the server its Transport::HTTP, which holds the server strongly), although the Upstream object itself is freed since k73. k93's t/reload-free.t subtest therefore drives the class entries through /all only; once k92 is fixed, extend it to the per-server endpoint.
